<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">

    <!--
        Metrics shared by both surface families. Colour lives in Controls.Tavern.xaml,
        Controls.Parchment.xaml and Controls.Semantic.xaml, all of which derive from the styles
        below via BasedOn. A padding or radius change belongs here so it lands once rather than
        once per derived file.

        ADOPTION, as counted from call sites — two of the three derived files are live and one
        is not, and the split is not the one an earlier revision of this comment claimed:

          Controls.Parchment.xaml   LIVE. DmBorderParchmentStyle (CreateDungeonPopup.xaml:37
                                    and :55), DmEntryParchment (:40), DmEditorParchment (:58).
                                    DmButtonParchment, DmPickerParchment and DmLabelParchment
                                    have no call sites.
          Controls.Semantic.xaml    LIVE. DmConfirmButton (CreateDungeonPopup.xaml:82) and
                                    DmNeutralButton (:72). DmDestructiveButton has ZERO call
                                    sites anywhere in the repo — it is a style waiting for the
                                    destructive confirm dialog to adopt it, not a consumer.
          Controls.Tavern.xaml      ENTIRELY UNCONSUMED. All 8 keys, no call sites. Tavern-
                                    surface elements set their tokens directly instead.

        None of that is a defect to fix here. The unconsumed keys are a consequence of the
        pixel-identical constraint the theme migration ran under: adopting a paired style would
        have moved geometry. Adoption is scheduled for the geometry-homogenisation sweep, which
        is allowed to move pixels. Keep these counts correct if you add or remove a call site.
    -->

    <Style x:Key="DmButtonBase" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontTitle}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeAction}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource DmRadiusDefault}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderWidth" Value="{StaticResource DmStrokeHairline}" />
        <!-- Padding and the minimum size requests deliberately match the geometry-only
             implicit Button style in Implicit.xaml, so a themed button and an
             unstyled one settle on the same footprint instead of fighting each
             other. CornerRadius stays bound to DmRadiusDefault: a button that
             opts into a keyed style is opting into the themed 3px radius on
             purpose. -->
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,10" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumHeightRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="MinimumWidthRequest" Value="44" />
        <Setter Property="VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups">
            <VisualStateGroupList>
                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.82" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.7" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                    <!-- This Disabled state is deliberate and it is OPT-IN: a keyed style is
                         chosen at the call site, so a Button carrying one has asked for the
                         themed disabled treatment. Implicit.xaml has no Disabled state at all,
                         and its comment records why — a VisualState setter outranks BOTH a
                         DataTrigger and a local value, so an implicit one would silently
                         overrule every call site that dims itself.
                         The same precedence applies here: this 0.45 outranks anything a
                         consuming Button sets for itself. Nothing is affected today, because
                         both live consumers restate the group (DmConfirmButton) or can never be
                         disabled (DmNeutralButton on CANCEL). A future keyed style that wants
                         its own disabled appearance must restate this whole group, not add a
                         setter at the call site. -->
                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                        <VisualState.Setters>
                            <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45" />
                        </VisualState.Setters>
                    </VisualState>
                </VisualStateGroup>
            </VisualStateGroupList>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmEntryBase" TargetType="Entry">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmEditorBase" TargetType="Editor">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="AutoSize" Value="Disabled" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmPickerBase" TargetType="Picker">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBodySmall}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmSearchBarBase" TargetType="SearchBar">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmLabelBase" TargetType="Label">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource DmFontBody}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource DmFontSizeBody}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="DmBorderBase" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{StaticResource DmStrokeHairline}" />
        <!-- The 3 mirrors DmRadiusDefault and must move with it;
             BorderBaseStrokeShapeRadius_EqualsDmRadiusDefault enforces that. The STRING form
             used here cannot avoid the literal: its converter parses a shape name and its
             arguments out of one string and has nowhere to accept a markup extension.

             That limitation belongs to the string form only, and is easy to over-read as a
             limitation of StrokeShape. The element form puts the radius back in attribute
             position, where a StaticResource does work — see the note beside the radius tokens
             in Tokens.xaml. It is not used here because a Style setter taking an element-form
             shape shares one geometry instance across every Border that adopts the style, which
             is a behaviour change and needs a spike, not a search-and-replace.

             This setter is a default, not a constraint, and must not be removed on the grounds
             that it "forces" a radius. MAUI value precedence puts a locally-set property above
             a Style setter, so a Border may carry Style="{StaticResource DmBorderTavernStyle}"
             AND its own StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 4" attribute — or a <Border.StrokeShape>
             element carrying a custom geometry — and keep its own shape. Adopting a paired
             style alongside a different shape is intended usage; CreateDungeonPopup.xaml does
             exactly this. -->
        <Setter Property="StrokeShape" Value="RoundRectangle 3" />
        <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
